# README wording — YOURS. The scaffold creates this file once and never
# overwrites it.
#
# A slot set here words the generated README files over the same slot of
# main.kit.info, where apidef puts the spec's metadata. A slot left out keeps
# the spec's wording. Text edited into a generated README is lost on the next
# generate; text here is not.
#
#   main: kit: text: {
#     tagline: 'Payments, invoices and refunds from TypeScript, Go and Python.'
#     summary: 'Acme Pay is a payments API for marketplaces.'
#     entity_desc: { invoice: 'A bill sent to a customer.' }
#   }
#
# The slots are title, summary, website, tagline, about_md, homepage,
# docs_url, meta_source and entity_desc. Any other key fails the model build.

# Written in our own words: the summary, the about text and the entity
# descriptions do not quote Artil's documentation.
main: kit: text: {
  title: 'Artil API'
  summary: 'Artil gives AI agents their own email inboxes, SMS numbers, Telegram bots and Slack apps, and a Message URL where other agents and teammates reach them. Its HTTP API manages agents, their addresses and access rules, sends and reads their messages, and delivers new ones by webhook.'
  website: 'https://artil.ai'
  docs_url: 'https://docs.artil.ai/api'
  about_md: `**Credentials.** Every call takes a bearer token, passed as the \`apikey\` option; the SDK
sends it as \`Authorization: Bearer ...\`. An agent's token (made in the Artil app on the agent's
Tokens tab) acts as that agent: it reads and sends the agent's messages and manages its
addresses, access and webhooks. Creating or deleting an agent needs a workspace member's
sign-in and refuses an agent's token. \`Me\` load says which the token is. The base URL is
\`https://app.artil.ai/api\`. The CLI and the MCP server read the token from \`ARTIL_APIKEY\`
(Artil's own CLI reads the same token from \`ARTIL_TOKEN\`), so an agent's MCP config sets it
under \`env\`: \`"env": { "ARTIL_APIKEY": "..." }\`.

Resources under an agent take the agent's id as \`agent_id\`. It is the last part of the
agent's Message URL, \`https://artil.ai/a/<agent-id>\`.

\`\`\`python
import os
from artil_sdk import ArtilSDK

client = ArtilSDK({"apikey": os.environ.get("ARTIL_APIKEY", "")})
agent_id = os.environ.get("ARTIL_AGENT_ID", "YOUR_AGENT_ID")

# Who this token signs in as: a member, or an agent.
print(client.Me().load().data_get().get("kind"))

# The agent's email addresses, phone numbers and bots.
for address in client.Address().list({"agent_id": agent_id}):
    print(address.data_get().get("channel"), address.data_get().get("value"))

# Its latest inbound messages, newest first.
for message in client.Message().list(
    {"agent_id": agent_id, "direction": "inbound", "limit": 10}
):
    print(message.data_get().get("counterpart"), message.data_get().get("subject"))
\`\`\`

Sending an email from the agent's primary address, in TypeScript:

\`\`\`ts
import { ArtilSDK } from '@voxgig-sdk/artil-sdk'

const client = new ArtilSDK({ apikey: process.env.ARTIL_APIKEY ?? '' })
const agent_id = process.env.ARTIL_AGENT_ID ?? 'YOUR_AGENT_ID'

const sent = await client.Message().create({
  agent_id,
  channel: 'email',
  to: ['someone@example.com'],
  subject: 'Hello',
  text: 'A first email from the agent.',
})
console.log(sent.data().id)
\`\`\`

**Sending.** \`Message\` create starts a message with a \`channel\` and recipients in \`to\`,
answers one when given its id as \`replyTo\`, and passes one on when given its id as
\`forward\` plus recipients. Attachments go in \`attachments\`: a file the agent already has,
by its \`id\`, or a text file written inline as \`name\`, \`type\` and \`text\`. \`Event\` create
sends to the agent's Message URL instead: the message goes straight to whichever client
listens to the agent, and Artil keeps no copy.

**Paging.** Lists answer plain arrays. Messages come newest first, at most 50 at a time
(\`limit\`); pass the last one's id as \`before\` for the page before it. Events come newest
first; pass an event's id as \`after\` for the ones that followed it, oldest first.

**Files.** Sending a file from disk takes multipart form data, which this generation does not
send. Text files go inline and files the agent has go by id, as above; every other call is
JSON both ways.

**Scope.** 32 of the 33 operations in Artil's published definition are here, as 15 entities.
The one left out, \`GET /agents/{id}/events/stream\`, holds a server-sent event stream open,
which this generation does not read. \`Event\` list with \`after\`, or a webhook, gets the same
events.

**Not yet run with a token.** Every request was checked against the definition, not against
Artil.`

  entity_desc: {
    access: 'Who can wake an agent on each channel: read the rules for every channel, or set the rule for one (its `id` is the channel: email, sms, telegram, slack or url).'
    address: "An agent's email address, phone number, Telegram bot or Slack bot: list, add, read, change or remove one, or make it the one its channel sends from (`primary`)."
    address_setup: 'A link where a workspace member creates a Telegram bot or a Slack app for an agent with the provider.'
    agent: 'An agent account in the workspace: list, create, read, rename or change the icon of one, or delete it with its addresses and messages.'
    channel: 'The channels an agent can send and receive on, with the labels and the size limits of each.'
    client: 'The clients that can act as an agent: CLI and OAuth sign-ins, and tokens once they have been used.'
    connection: "The client that hears an agent's new messages: read it, or make the calling client that one (an agent's own token only)."
    event: "An agent's events, such as a message arriving: list them, or send a message to the agent's Message URL (create)."
    listener: "Who hears an agent's new messages now: its connected client and its webhooks."
    me: 'The member or agent the calling token signs in as, and its workspace.'
    me_token: 'The calling token itself: remove it to revoke it.'
    message: 'An email, text, Telegram or Slack message an agent received or sent: list them (by channel, direction or counterpart), read one, or send one, which can answer (`replyTo`) or pass on (`forward`) another.'
    teammate: "The workspace's agents and members, with the addresses that reach each, and whether anyone listens to each agent."
    token: 'The tokens members made for an agent, never their values: list them or revoke one.'
    webhook: "Where an agent's events are posted: list, add or remove one. Adding one returns its signing secret."
  }
}
